erence/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/state.json" "file_sha256" "77cf74403585a2158aafb51f1ab8af2700f117e59e984fd0ad6fda4b3ab7c285"} {"question" "q-78337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "Dilation with scale factor $17$"] ["text" "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{1}{17}$"] ["text" "Reflection across the $x$-axis"] ["text" "Reflection across the $y$-axis"] ["text" "Reflection in the line $x=17$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/state.json" "file_sha256" "77cf74403585a2158aafb51f1ab8af2700f117e59e984fd0ad6fda4b3ab7c285"} {"question" "q-78337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "Dilation with scale factor $17$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/state.json" "file_sha256" "77cf74403585a2158aafb51f1ab8af2700f117e59e984fd0ad6fda4b3ab7c285"} {"question" "q-78299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following represents a reflection across the $y$-axis?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/state.json" "file_sha256" "77cf74403585a2158aafb51f1ab8af2700f117e59e984fd0ad6fda4b3ab7c285"} {"question" "q-78299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The correct answer is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Indeed, consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nNow, we can find the images of ${i,j}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/4993d8586a84e4a40a54edddd8011cd3d516264bfffc7e3c33d27a10edf1e7d7.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/state.json" "file_sha256" "77cf74403585a2158aafb51f1ab8af2700f117e59e984fd0ad6fda4b3ab7c285"} {"question" "q-78299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "01da23f653e5f65150b5911aeab7a13a958c3bb55c1d6e7287af659b69cfd5da"} {"question" "q-78299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/state.json" "file_sha256" "77cf74403585a2158aafb51f1ab8af2700f117e59e984fd0ad6fda4b3ab7c285"} {"question" "q-78299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/state.json" "file_sha256" "77cf74403585a2158aafb51f1ab8af2700f117e59e984fd0ad6fda4b3ab7c285"} {"question" "q-78299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/state.json" "file_sha256" "77cf74403585a2158aafb51f1ab8af2700f117e59e984fd0ad6fda4b3ab7c285"} {"question" "e-5452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a dilation. Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/bd6574ee05b60919de656a889f8532e5a512b381eae63f7a65f251a7afe72738.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/be73c2a933a6fb99eaa389774a618848bdadd9cafdf0fcd2f6af126b49b87707.png) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/example-5452.json" "file_sha256" "b32d811585eb55e66592e2b292ad2b8d56cbab7178cc5a0736aa7105deac9b4b"} {"question" "e-5452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can write $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nRemember that the matrix representing a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/4866597a50fbdb530e9275c77f2ca926c932c7cc334635bd05191aaddd510d75.png)\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/962b9458714f9b7f6b17fe2259e140dfabba443d8ecb5b9e2328172ab3f8a1a2.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/example-5452.json" "file_sha256" "b32d811585eb55e66592e2b292ad2b8d56cbab7178cc5a0736aa7105deac9b4b"} {"question" "e-5451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What linear transformation is represented by the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/6191f617d2c2e89e07c1cc349a5926874f11ea78d046185ce990e566225a7c79.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/example-5451.json" "file_sha256" "f58babbf7cbd462bb0de0238999a5c78f738f9ac4894ac7e752408616a28cce4"} {"question" "e-5451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as depicted below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents the reflection across the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/example-5451.json" "file_sha256" "f58babbf7cbd462bb0de0238999a5c78f738f9ac4894ac7e752408616a28cce4"}] "usage" {"e-5451" [] "e-5452" [] "q-78299" []} "basis" 1763 "retractions" [[17592186087503 :question/problem "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a dilation. Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$?"] [17592186087503 :question/worked-solution "We can write $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(v) &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ is a dilation with scale factor\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nRemember that the matrix representing a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186087502 :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186087502 :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as depicted below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/867/Source/Images/a075449f9d98c2f5b4e14b915f2365f2.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ni^{′} &= T(i) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = i \\\\\nj^{′} &= T(j) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} =-j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/867/Source/Images/45b1b054a2d140d9a815f9b4816138e7.png)\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents the reflection across the $x$-axis."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186086749 17592186086750 17592186086751 17592186086752 17592186086753] "immutable_fields" [[17592186086748 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086748 :answer-field/id] [17592186086748 :answer-field/key] [17592186086748 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
